 ## Background
-Hard Voxelization is a fundamental technique in point cloud processing, originating from the need to convert unstructured 3D data into a regular grid format for convolutional neural networks (CNNs) and volume rendering [S1, unofficial][S4]. The method is adapted from functions like `voxels()` originally written by J. Antonio Guzmán Q. for the `rTLS` package [S4]. It is widely used in autonomous driving datasets (e.g., nuScenes, KITTI) for 3D object detection [S1, S3, unofficial]. The deterministic variant was developed to address the non-reproducibility of atomic-based GPU implementations, ensuring consistent results across different hardware and software versions [S3, unofficial].
+Hard Voxelization is a fundamental technique in point cloud processing, originating from the need to convert unstructured 3D data into a regular grid format for convolutional neural networks (CNNs) and volume rendering [S1, unofficial][S4, unofficial]. The method is adapted from functions like `voxels()` originally written by J. Antonio Guzmán Q. for the `rTLS` package [S4, unofficial]. It is used for benchmarking point cloud downsampling on the nuScenes dataset [S4, unofficial]. The deterministic variant was developed to address the non-reproducibility of atomic-based GPU implementations, ensuring consistent results across different hardware and software versions [S3, unofficial].
 
